      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into a zoning issue in Austin regarding a parcel currently zoned R-2 that we want to reclassify to Mixed-Use (MU). We've run into some conflicting language in the municipal code regarding setback requirements and how they interact with existing encroachments from neighboring properties. Can you help me outline a strategy for navigating these conflicting clauses when filing for the reclassification?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When addressing this zoning issue, it's essential to thoroughly review the Riverside (R-2) district regulations, as well as the Mixed-Use (MU) proposed district guidelines in Austin's Municipal Code. Begin by researching precedent cases in Austin where R-2 zones have been successfully transitioned to Mixed-Use, paying close attention to how setback requirements were handled.\n\n\nNext, you should map any existing encroachments on the neighboring properties, documenting them comprehensively. This information will be vital when arguing that the specific conditions do not have a material impact on the viability of the Mixed-Use reclassification, aiming to minimize alterations required for the rezoning approval.\n\n\nConsult with an urban planner or land-use attorney to interpret the conflicting language in the municipal code and draft a narrative that addresses potential concerns. In the filing, plainly articulate your proposal for compartmentalized zoning, highlighting how the refuge can maintain the parcels' functional integrity despite the setback concerns.\n\n\nFinally, engage with the community and local stakeholders to gain support for your proposal, which can be instrumental during the public hearing. By demonstrating broad support, you're more likely to sway the decision in your favor.",
      "source": "target",
